\begin_layout Standard
Genus synthesize the design in 3 phases: Generic, map and final.
 Generic synthesis is the initial phase where the RTL (e.g., Verilog or VHDL)
 is converted into a technology-independent representation using generic
 logic gates like AND, OR, MUX, and DFF.
 Mapping is the process of transforming the generic netlist into a technology-sp
ecific netlist using a standard cell library (e.g., 45nm or 7nm cells).
 Incremental synthesis is an iterative or localized re-synthesis approach
 applied when changes are made to a portion of the design (e.g., ECOs or constrain
t changes), without re-optimizing the entire design.
\end_layout

